skill: Database Design and Optimization

@ Normalization | B
What is the main goal of database normalization?
= Reduce data redundancy and improve data integrity
- Make every query run faster
- Encrypt the stored data
- Increase the number of tables as much as possible
? Normalization organizes data so each fact is stored once, which avoids update, insert and delete anomalies.

@ Normalization | B
A table is in First Normal Form (1NF) when:
= Every column holds atomic values and there are no repeating groups
- It has no partial dependencies on a composite key
- It has no transitive dependencies
- It has at least one index
? 1NF requires single, indivisible values in each cell and no repeating groups of columns.

@ Normalization | I
Which normal form removes partial dependencies on part of a composite primary key?
= Second Normal Form (2NF)
- First Normal Form (1NF)
- Third Normal Form (3NF)
- Fourth Normal Form (4NF)
? 2NF requires every non-key column to depend on the whole primary key, not just a part of it.

@ Normalization | I
Which normal form removes transitive dependencies between non-key columns?
= Third Normal Form (3NF)
- First Normal Form (1NF)
- Second Normal Form (2NF)
- Zero Normal Form
? In 3NF, non-key columns depend only on the key, not on other non-key columns.

@ Indexing | B
What is the primary purpose of a database index?
= Speed up the retrieval of rows
- Reduce the disk space used by the table
- Enforce foreign key relationships
- Encrypt column values
? An index works like a book index and lets the database locate rows without scanning the whole table.

@ Indexing | I
What is a common downside of creating too many indexes on a table?
= INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE become slower and storage use grows
- SELECT queries always become slower
- The indexes delete old data automatically
- The table can no longer have a primary key
? Every write must also update each index, so indexes trade write speed and space for read speed.

@ Indexing | I
Which column is the best candidate for an index?
= A column often used in WHERE or JOIN conditions that has many distinct values
- A column with only two distinct values that is never filtered on
- Every column of the table
- A large text column that is never queried
? Indexes help most on selective columns that queries actually use.

@ Indexing | A
A composite index exists on (last_name, first_name). Which query can use it efficiently?
= WHERE last_name = 'Rao'
- WHERE first_name = 'Anil' only
- Both queries use it equally well
- Neither query can use it
? A composite index follows the leftmost-prefix rule, so the query must filter on the leading column.

@ Transactions and ACID | B
In the ACID properties, what does the letter A stand for?
= Atomicity
- Availability
- Accuracy
- Authorization
? Atomicity means a transaction either completes entirely or has no effect at all.

@ Transactions and ACID | I
Which ACID property guarantees that committed data survives a system crash?
= Durability
- Isolation
- Consistency
- Atomicity
? Durability means that once a transaction commits, its changes are permanently stored.

@ Transactions and ACID | I
Which SQL command undoes the changes made in the current transaction?
= ROLLBACK
- UNDO
- REVERT
- CANCEL
? ROLLBACK discards uncommitted changes. COMMIT makes them permanent.

@ Transactions and ACID | A
Which problem occurs when a transaction reads data that another transaction has changed but not yet committed?
= Dirty read
- Phantom read
- Deadlock
- Index scan
? A dirty read sees uncommitted changes that may later be rolled back. Higher isolation levels prevent it.

@ Views and Stored Programs | B
What is a view in SQL?
= A stored query that behaves like a virtual table
- A physical copy of a table
- A type of index
- A backup file
? A view stores a SELECT definition and returns fresh results each time it is queried.

@ Views and Stored Programs | I
Which is a benefit of using stored procedures?
= Reusable server-side logic that can reduce network round trips
- They make every query faster automatically
- They remove the need for backups
- They replace table design
? A stored procedure runs inside the database, so related statements execute in a single call.

@ Query Optimization | B
Which statement shows how MySQL plans to execute a query?
= EXPLAIN
- SHOW TABLES
- USE
- TRUNCATE
? EXPLAIN displays the execution plan, such as which indexes are used and how tables are joined.

@ Query Optimization | A
Why can WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2025 be slow on a large table even if order_date is indexed?
= Wrapping the column in a function prevents the index from being used
- YEAR is deprecated in SQL
- Indexes only work on string columns
- WHERE cannot contain functions
? Functions applied to an indexed column hide it from the optimizer. A range such as order_date >= '2025-01-01' AND order_date < '2026-01-01' can use the index.

@ Backup and Security | B
Which security principle says users should get only the permissions they need?
= Least privilege
- Maximum access
- Role inversion
- Open access
? Granting minimal permissions limits the damage from mistakes or compromised accounts.

@ Backup and Security | I
Which statement gives a MySQL user permission to read a table?
= GRANT SELECT ON shop.orders TO 'report'@'localhost';
- ALLOW SELECT ON shop.orders TO report;
- PERMIT READ shop.orders FOR report;
- GIVE SELECT shop.orders report;
? GRANT assigns privileges. REVOKE removes them.

@ Backup and Security | I
Which backup type copies the data that changed since the last FULL backup?
= Differential backup
- Full backup
- Snapshot of the schema only
- Transaction rename
? A differential backup grows over time until the next full backup. An incremental backup copies changes since the last backup of any kind.
